+# Heat-Safety Brief for Family Caregivers
+
+## Audience and Scope
+
+This brief is for family caregivers of adults age 65 and older only. It uses the supplied evidence packet to support three actions: proactive check-ins, access to a cool place, and a medication or health-condition discussion with a pharmacist or clinician. It is general information, not an individual prediction or clinical assessment.
+
+## Sourced Facts
+
+Adults age 65 and older are more prone to heat-related health problems and may adjust less well to sudden temperature changes. Chronic conditions and prescription medicines can affect temperature control or sweating. The CDC advises caregivers to visit at least twice daily during hot weather and watch for heat illness. It advises drinking more water than usual, while telling people with fluid limits or water pills to ask their doctor how much to drink `[CDC Older Adults]`.
+
+The CDC identifies an air-conditioned building as a primary protective step and advises contacting the local health department or locating an air-conditioned shelter when a home lacks cooling. A fan should not be the main cooling device during extreme heat `[CDC Older Adults]`.
+
+NIOSH lists headache, nausea, dizziness, weakness, irritability, thirst, heavy sweating, elevated temperature, and decreased urine output as possible heat-exhaustion signs. Heat-stroke signs include confusion, altered mental status, slurred speech, loss of consciousness, seizures, and very high temperature. Without emergency treatment, heat stroke can cause death or permanent disability `[NIOSH Heat Illness]`.
+
+The heat index combines temperature and humidity, but its standard value assumes shade and light wind. Full sunshine can add up to 15 degrees Fahrenheit. Alert criteria vary by location; a forecast does not describe a home’s indoor temperature. Warm nights can reduce recovery `[NWS Heat Tools]`.
+
+## Recommendations
+
+The combined three-part approach is a recommendation synthesized from the packet; it is not a program the packet directly tested. First, create a check-in plan before hot weather: agree on who will call or visit at least twice daily and what follows a missed contact. Ask about comfort, indoor heat, drinking, and symptoms rather than assuming silence means safety.
+
+Second, identify a cool place and how to reach it. Confirm hours, accessibility, transportation, and a backup. An air-conditioned home or community building fits the guidance; a fan alone does not replace it in extreme heat `[CDC Older Adults]`. Use forecasts, alerts, and hot nights as triggers to check actual conditions, not as a personal diagnosis `[NWS Heat Tools]`.
+
+Third, discuss chronic conditions, fluid restrictions, water pills, and other prescriptions with a pharmacist or clinician before hot weather. Caregivers and older adults should not change, stop, skip, or adjust medication on their own. This conversation supplies individualized instructions the packet cannot.
+
+## Action Table
+
+| Action | Who acts | When | Why |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Before hot weather | Family caregiver with the older adult | Before the first expected hot period | Agree on contacts, clinician questions, cooling locations, transport, and backups while there is time to solve gaps. |
+| During a heat alert | Family caregiver | When the local weather office issues an alert, and during warm nights | Check the forecast and the person’s actual indoor conditions; local alerts describe an area, not a home or individual risk `[NWS Heat Tools]`. |
+| At each check-in | Family caregiver | At least twice daily during hot weather | Ask about symptoms, fluids, and access to cooling; CDC specifically recommends twice-daily visits and observation `[CDC Older Adults]`. |
+| If emergency signs appear | Family caregiver or anyone present | Immediately after confusion, slurred speech, loss of consciousness, seizures, or other suspected heat-stroke signs | Call 911, move the person to a cool shaded area, cool quickly, and stay with them because heat stroke is an emergency `[NIOSH Heat Illness]`. |
+
+## Uncertainty and Limits
+
+Important uncertainty remains. Group-level risk does not predict an individual response: conditions, medicines, housing, and exposure differ. The packet cannot determine one person’s fluid or care needs; a pharmacist or clinician must provide that guidance.
+
+The heat index assumes shade and light wind; sunshine may feel hotter, and an area forecast cannot establish indoor temperature. Check the actual setting rather than infer it from one number `[NWS Heat Tools]`.
+
+The packet does not compare check-in schedules or evaluate the combined approach. Twice-daily visiting is CDC guidance, but the documents do not establish the best frequency or contact method for every situation `[CDC Older Adults]`.
+
+The packet does not identify local cooling resources, transport, hours, accessibility, or outage backups. Confirm them locally. Symptom lists overlap and cannot diagnose severity remotely `[NIOSH Heat Illness]`.
+
+## Emergency Signs
+
+Treat confusion, altered mental status, slurred speech, loss of consciousness, seizures, or suspected heat stroke as an emergency. Call 911, move the person to a shaded cool area, cool them quickly, and do not leave the person alone `[NIOSH Heat Illness]`. For possible heat exhaustion, leave the heat, cool the person, offer liquids if appropriate, stay, and obtain medical evaluation; call 911 if care is unavailable. If signs are unclear, seek professional help rather than waiting for certainty.
